From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 12:20:31 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] vhost: do not verify vring host mappings under Xen, it
 abort()s the device model

qemu 11.1.2 port of
meta-xt-qemu/recipes-qemu/qemu/qemu/0119-vhost-do-not-verify-vring-host-mappings-under-Xen.patch;
that patch's description has the reasoning and the measurements.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending [not yet submitted to qemu-devel; the check it skips cannot succeed under Xen, where qemu never maps the whole of a guest's RAM. Note that the vhost backends this unblocks get their Xen address translation from the fork's own guest-side vhost-Xen work, which upstream does not have]
---
 hw/virtio/vhost.c | 28 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 28 insertions(+)

diff --git a/hw/virtio/vhost.c b/hw/virtio/vhost.c
index 371dca17d..74e53a066 100644
--- a/hw/virtio/vhost.c
+++ b/hw/virtio/vhost.c
@@ -29,6 +29,7 @@
 #include "system/dma.h"
 #include "system/memory.h"
 #include "system/ramblock.h"
+#include "system/xen.h"
 #include "trace.h"
 
 /* enabled until disconnected backend stabilizes */
@@ -586,6 +587,33 @@ static int vhost_verify_ring_mappings(struct vhost_dev *dev,
         return 0;
     }
 
+    /*
+     * Under Xen there is nothing here to verify, and verifying anyway is fatal.
+     *
+     * qemu does not map the whole of a Xen guest's RAM: the RAMBlock backing
+     * "xen.ram" has host == NULL and the vrings are reached on demand through
+     * the map cache (xen_map_cache()). memory_region_get_ram_ptr() therefore
+     * returns NULL for those sections, so the userspace_addr that
+     * vhost_commit() records in the vhost memory table is a pseudo-hva equal to
+     * the guest physical address -- while vq->desc holds a real map-cache
+     * pointer. The "ring's MemoryRegion wasn't replaced" comparison below can
+     * then never hold, so every ring looks "relocated" and vhost_commit()
+     * abort()s the device model on the FIRST memory topology change that
+     * happens after the device was started. vhost_dev_start() commits before it
+     * sets ->started, which is the only reason this is not hit at boot.
+     *
+     * On Arm that first post-start change is a virtio-gpu host-visible blob
+     * being mapped into the guest (Venus/Vulkan), so enabling Vulkan in a guest
+     * killed its device model as soon as Mesa created its first Venus context.
+     * Address translation for these backends is done by domid rather than by
+     * host VA, so the mapping identity this function tries to police is not the
+     * one actually in use. Skip it; vhost_commit() still updates the memory
+     * table itself below.
+     */
+    if (xen_enabled()) {
+        return 0;
+    }
+
     for (i = 0; i < dev->nvqs; ++i) {
         struct vhost_virtqueue *vq = dev->vqs + i;
 
